# Backend environment template. See guides/CONNECTIONS.md for service ownership.
# Local backend: root .env. Production backend: Railway tender-laughter/docxy.
# The Next.js dashboard instead reads web/.env.local or Vercel variables.
# Never assume copying this file configures both applications.

# ---- Nebius Token Factory ---------------------------------------------------
# Get a key at https://tokenfactory.nebius.com
NEBIUS_API_KEY=

# Defaults are fine; override only if you need a different region or provider name.
# NEBIUS_BASE_URL=https://api.tokenfactory.nebius.com/v1
# NEBIUS_PROVIDER_NAME=nebius

# Upstream model ids registered with the harness. Model ids move - run
# `docxy models` to see what your account can actually serve, then point
# these at real ids and re-run `docxy setup`.
# NEBIUS_MODEL_PRIMARY=deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Pro      # 1M ctx, structured outputs
# NEBIUS_MODEL_FAST=deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash       # cheaper sibling
# NEBIUS_MODEL_KIMI=moonshotai/Kimi-K3
# NEBIUS_MODEL_QWEN=Qwen/Qwen3.5-397B-A17B

# ---- Which model each role uses --------------------------------------------
# Format: <provider-name>/<registered-model-name>
# Registered names are: deepseek-v4-pro, deepseek-v4-flash, kimi-k3, qwen3-5
# DOCXY_MODEL_COORDINATOR=nebius/deepseek-v4-pro
# DOCXY_MODEL_CHANGE_ANALYST=nebius/deepseek-v4-pro
# DOCXY_MODEL_IMPACT_MAPPER=nebius/deepseek-v4-pro
# DOCXY_MODEL_DOCS_UPDATER=nebius/deepseek-v4-pro
# Changelog Author defaults to deepseek-v4-pro. Only override this after
# verifying the alternate model reliably produces the required JSON.
# DOCXY_MODEL_CHANGELOG_AUTHOR=nebius/deepseek-v4-pro

# ---- The repository being documented ----------------------------------------
# DOCXY_REPO_PATH=.
# DOCXY_DOCS_ROOTS=docs,README.md,doc,website/docs
# DOCXY_CHANGELOG=CHANGELOG.md
# DOCXY_STATE_DIR=.docxy

# Branch the documentation lives on. When set, docs are read from a throwaway
# worktree at that branch's tip and pull requests are opened against it - the
# code checkout is never touched. Leave unset to keep docs beside the code.
# The branch must already exist; docxy will not create it for you.
# DOCXY_DOCS_BRANCH=docs

# Documentation that lives in a *different repository* from the code.
#
# A docs site, a handbook, or one central documentation repository fed by
# several services. The commit that starts a run belongs to the code
# repository; the pull request is opened against this one, based on its own
# default branch rather than the code repository's.
#
# On a deployment these are resolved per run from the project you connected in
# the dashboard, and nothing here needs setting. They are the local equivalent,
# for the CLI and for a single-repository install.
#
# The App must be installed on both repositories: one to read the docs from,
# the other to open the pull request against.
# DOCXY_DOCS_REPO=acme/handbook
# DOCXY_DOCS_REPO_PATH=/absolute/path/to/handbook   # local checkout of it
# DOCXY_DOCS_BASE_BRANCH=main                        # its default branch

# ---- Validation -------------------------------------------------------------
# DOCXY_VALIDATE=true
# DOCXY_CHECK_LINKS=true
# DOCXY_DOCS_BUILD_COMMAND=          # e.g. "npm run docs:build"
# DOCXY_TEST_COMMAND=                # e.g. "npm test"

# ---- Guardrails ---------------------------------------------------------------
# A commit diff is not trusted input. It is written by whoever opened the pull
# request, it can carry a committed key straight to a model provider, and it can
# carry a sentence addressed to the agent that reads it.

# Redact known credential shapes before anything reaches a model. On by default
# and free - the match is a regex, not a model call. Covers AWS, GCP, GitHub,
# GitLab, Stripe, OpenAI, Anthropic, HuggingFace, Slack and npm token formats,
# private key blocks, passwords inside connection strings, and quoted
# credential assignments.
# DOCXY_REDACT_SECRETS=true

# Refuse a diff that is trying to give the agents instructions. Off by default:
# it costs one model call per run and its judgement is a model's. Turn it on for
# repositories that accept pull requests from outside your team.
#
# It fails open - if the detecting model errors, the content is allowed through
# and a warning is logged - so pick a model that reliably returns structured
# output. DeepSeek-V4-Flash does not, and fails open on every run.
# DOCXY_DETECT_PROMPT_INJECTION=false
# DOCXY_INJECTION_MODEL=nebius/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Pro
# DOCXY_INJECTION_THRESHOLD=0.7

# ---- Agent loop -------------------------------------------------------------
# Attempts per role, including the first. A failed attempt is retried on its own
# terms: a budget breach starts over on a fresh session, an unparseable answer is
# shown back to the model that wrote it, a rate limit backs off.
# DOCXY_ROLE_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
#
# Wall-clock ceiling for one attempt, in seconds. A role that stops answering
# fails its attempt instead of hanging the run.
# DOCXY_ROLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=420
#
# Turns a session may carry before it is retired and rebuilt.
#
# Session reuse is what makes the second commit cheaper than the first, but the
# transcript is also an input that grows without bound - an overfull session is
# what produced every `max_tokens breached` failure this pipeline has had. Set
# to 0 to keep sessions forever, at that risk.
# DOCXY_SESSION_MAX_TURNS=12
#
# Let the Docs Updater draft by writing a program instead of quoting from memory.
#
# Off by default. In this mode the role does not receive the documentation as
# text to reproduce: it writes one TypeScript program, reads each impacted file
# through a tool, locates the stale passage with ordinary string work, and hands
# back the bytes it found as the anchor. An anchor is then something measured
# rather than remembered, and a wrong one is reported inside the same call
# instead of after the turn has ended.
#
# It costs more than the prose variant - an agentic turn, several rounds, a
# sandboxed process - so it is off until it earns that against `docxy eval`.
# DOCXY_CODE_MODE=false
#
# Where that program runs. `daytona` (the default) or `local`.
#
# Daytona needs DAYTONA_API_KEY and is the only answer for a deployment: the
# workspace has no host filesystem and no egress, both measured - a probe inside
# one could not resolve a hostname, could not read /root, and found no /Users.
# A missing key is an error rather than a quiet fall back to this machine, because
# which host executes a model's code should not be decided by a variable somebody
# forgot to set. This is independent of DOCXY_SANDBOX, which only turns the docs
# build on and off.
#
# `local` runs the program here under seatbelt (macOS) or bubblewrap (Linux). The
# network is denied there too, but macOS cannot restrict filesystem reads - so it
# is a development setting, and it has to be asked for by name.
# DOCXY_CODE_MODE_SANDBOX=daytona
#
# Run a local program even where the platform offers no isolation.
#
# Only consulted for DOCXY_CODE_MODE_SANDBOX=local. Without an isolation backend
# code mode refuses rather than executing model-authored code on this machine;
# this is the override, kept separate because wanting the feature and accepting
# arbitrary code on this host are different decisions.
# DOCXY_CODE_MODE_FORCE_UNSANDBOXED=false

# ---- Pull requests ----------------------------------------------------------
# Docxy publishes only as its GitHub App. There is no personal-token or gh CLI
# fallback: a machine proposal opened under a human's name is the thing the bot
# identity exists to prevent. guides/GITHUB-APP.md registers the App and finds
# all three ids below.
# GITHUB_APP_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=
#
# The App's private key, from either source. Set both and the inline one wins.
#
# In THIS file, use the path. A PEM pasted here across its own lines does not
# work: the reader is line-based, so it keeps `-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----`
# and nothing else - and the base64 lines below it, the ones ending in `=`, get
# read as environment variables of their own. The failure is a key that will not
# parse, with nothing pointing at the paste.
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=/absolute/path/to/app.pem
#
# Deployed, prefer GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY: a managed platform hands a service
# environment variables, not a filesystem to place a secret on beforehand. It
# accepts the PEM verbatim (fine in Railway's or Vercel's multi-line editor),
# base64 of it, or one whose newlines survived as literal \n. To use it here in
# a single line, base64 it first:
#
#   base64 -i ~/.docxy/app.pem | tr -d '\n'     # macOS
#   base64 -w0 ~/.docxy/app.pem                  # Linux
# GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
# GITHUB_APP_SLUG=docxy
# GITHUB_APP_BOT_EMAIL=         # <user-id>+<slug>[bot]@users.noreply.github.com

# Required before POST /webhook accepts anything: openssl rand -hex 32
# GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Which repositories docxy documents is decided by the projects connected in the
# dashboard - one project, one repository - and /webhook checks each delivery
# against them, re-read per delivery. Installing the App is an access grant and
# nothing more: choosing "All repositories" does not sign them all up, and no
# push is documented until somebody connects that repository as a project.
#
# This variable only ever NARROWS that set, for a deployment that wants to
# document fewer repositories than are connected. It cannot widen it: a name
# nobody connected still starts no runs. Leave it unset unless you mean to
# exclude something.
# DOCXY_ALLOWED_REPOS=owner/repo,owner/other-repo

# DOCXY_BASE_BRANCH=main

# ---- Sandbox ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The docs build is the one validation step that runs a command over text a
# model just wrote, so it runs inside a Daytona workspace rather than on this
# machine. The files are uploaded over the SDK and the exit code is the
# process's own, so nothing about the result rests on a model's account of it.
#
# On by default, and it needs DAYTONA_API_KEY. Without one the build is reported
# unvalidated rather than run here - see DOCXY_SANDBOX_FALLBACK below.
# DOCXY_SANDBOX=true
# DAYTONA_API_KEY=

# What happens when the sandbox cannot run the build. "skip" (the default)
# fails the check and says why; the proposal opens as a draft carrying the
# reason. "local" runs the command on this machine instead - more coverage, and
# model-authored content touching your filesystem. Host execution is opt-in on
# purpose: a boundary that vanishes when unobserved is not a boundary.
# DOCXY_SANDBOX_FALLBACK=skip

# Runs inside the sandbox over the proposed docs. The sandbox receives the
# proposed markdown and NOTHING ELSE - no repository, no package.json, no
# node_modules. So this must work on markdown alone: a linter, a fence check, a
# link checker. `mkdocs build` and `npm run docs:build` need a tree that is not
# there and will report that they could not run.
# DOCXY_DOCS_BUILD_COMMAND=

# How long Daytona keeps a sandbox around. 0 disables the timer.
# The workspace is ephemeral and is deleted when it stops; this is the backstop
# for a process that dies before it can clean up after itself.
# DOCXY_SANDBOX_AUTOSTOP_MINUTES=15
# DOCXY_SANDBOX_EXEC_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=600

# Let the docs build reach the network from inside the workspace.
#
# Off by default. The command runs over prose a model wrote minutes ago, and a
# sandbox that can still reach the network is a sandbox in name only. Turn it on
# for a build that genuinely needs a package registry, and know that you have.
# DOCXY_SANDBOX_ALLOW_NETWORK=false

# How much documentation text is inlined into one Docs Updater prompt, in
# characters, shared across every impacted file.
#
# The Docs Updater must quote its find anchors verbatim from the text it is
# given, so a budget too small to carry the impacted files is a budget that
# guarantees invented anchors. Files that still will not fit are named to the
# model as ones it must skip rather than dropped in silence.
# DOCXY_DOC_EXCERPT_BUDGET=200000

# DOCXY_PORT=4317

# ---- API authentication ------------------------------------------------------
# The shared secret the dashboard presents to this API. The API answers approve,
# publish, run and instructions, so a caller that can reach the port can publish a
# proposal - Better Auth on the dashboard does not cover this door.
#
# `docxy serve` binds 127.0.0.1 and may run without it. The deployed entry point
# (npm start) binds 0.0.0.0 and refuses to boot without it. Set the same value
# here and in the dashboard's environment.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# DOCXY_API_TOKEN=

# Bind `docxy serve` somewhere other than loopback. Only do this with
# DOCXY_API_TOKEN set - it puts the approval endpoints on your network.
# DOCXY_HOST=127.0.0.1

# ---- Persistence -------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset, the pipeline keeps runs, sessions, and the symbol map as JSON in
# .docxy/ - zero setup, and what the demo uses. Set it and all three move to
# Postgres instead. Use Neon's *pooled* connection string (the one with
# `-pooler`). See guides/DATABASE.md.
#
# Apply the schema once with:  npm run db:migrate
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@ep-xxx-pooler.region.aws.neon.tech/docxy?sslmode=require

# ---- Dashboard (web/) --------------------------------------------------------
# The Next.js dashboard reads its own web/.env.local - see web/.env.local.example.
# It shares DATABASE_URL with the pipeline but keeps Better Auth's tables in a
# separate `auth` schema.
#
#   DATABASE_URL          same Neon database as above
#   BETTER_AUTH_SECRET    openssl rand -base64 32
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID / GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET     optional social sign-in
#   GITHUB_CLIENT_ID / GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET     optional social sign-in
#   DOCXY_API_TOKEN       the same value as above
#   COMPOSIO_API_KEY      account connection setup; WEB environment only
#
# Authorization is by organization membership. DOCXY_ALLOWED_EMAILS and
# DOCXY_ALLOW_SIGNUP are legacy variables, no longer read. Registration uses
# verified email and requires working Resend configuration on the dashboard.
#
# The demo escape hatch is DOCXY_REQUIRE_AUTH=0, which turns the check off
# altogether and is explicit about what it gives up. Missing DATABASE_URL or
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET is *not* an escape hatch: that is a misconfigured
# deployment, and it refuses requests rather than serving them to anyone.
#
# Set these in web/.env.local, not in this backend template:
#   DOCXY_API_URL=http://localhost:4317
#   COMPOSIO_API_KEY=<your project key>
# In Vercel production, DOCXY_API_URL=https://docxy-production.up.railway.app.
# The Composio key enables account authorization controls after the web code is
# deployed; it does not enable automatic notifications, publishing or issues.

# ---- Billing (Dodo Payments) -------------------------------------------------
# Configured on the dashboard, not here - see web/.env.local.example for the
# full list. This side owns the tables: `npm run db:migrate` from the repository
# root creates the `billing` schema, and the dashboard reads and writes it
# through the same DATABASE_URL. Nothing in the pipeline contacts Dodo.
#
# With no Dodo credentials anywhere, the product runs with payments switched
# off. That is the self-hosted case and it stays supported.
